1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,673
Hi everyone,

2
00:00:01,693 --> 00:00:08,228
and welcome back to Sewing Machine Design and Manufacturing at WenQuest.

3
00:00:08,628 --> 00:00:16,739
This is Lecture 13, control basics, with motors, drives and sensors.

4
00:00:17,139 --> 00:00:23,163
Today we move into electrical control, with two questions running through it.

5
00:00:23,183 --> 00:00:26,852
How does the shaft stop precisely at needle-up?

6
00:00:26,872 --> 00:00:32,661
And how many degrees of shaft rotation do the actuators' delays amount to?

7
00:00:34,181 --> 00:00:35,521
The shop-floor problem.

8
00:00:35,921 --> 00:00:39,001
To shorten the stop at the end of each seam,

9
00:00:39,021 --> 00:00:43,864
a sewing floor raised the trimming speed of its electronic lockstitch

10
00:00:43,884 --> 00:00:46,048
machines from 400 to 600 r/min,

11
00:00:46,068 --> 00:00:48,021
and trimming became erratic.

12
00:00:48,421 --> 00:00:56,040
The trimming solenoid has an 8-ohm coil, a 24-volt boost then 1-amp hold,

13
00:00:56,060 --> 00:01:00,226
and a pull-in delay of 6.9 milliseconds;

14
00:01:00,246 --> 00:01:06,191
with diode freewheeling it releases in 12.9 milliseconds,

15
00:01:06,211 --> 00:01:09,121
with a 48-volt Zener in 5.1.

16
00:01:09,521 --> 00:01:16,957
The mechanism needs both pull-in and release within 20 degrees of shaft angle.

17
00:01:17,357 --> 00:01:21,234
What's wrong, and what's the highest trimming speed?

18
00:01:22,754 --> 00:01:27,534
What's inside an electronic lockstitch machine.

19
00:01:29,054 --> 00:01:34,560
An electronic lockstitch machine has three kinds of parts: sensors,

20
00:01:34,580 --> 00:01:37,034
the controller, and actuators.

21
00:01:37,434 --> 00:01:43,471
The sensors are the shaft encoder, the pedal, thread-break detection,

22
00:01:43,491 --> 00:01:48,738
bobbin-thread monitoring, fabric thickness and edge sensors.

23
00:01:49,138 --> 00:01:53,918
The controller holds the servo drive and the control program.

24
00:01:54,318 --> 00:02:01,469
The actuators are the main-shaft servo, the trimming and reverse solenoids,

25
00:02:01,489 --> 00:02:06,538
the foot-lift cylinder and the stitch-length stepper.

26
00:02:06,938 --> 00:02:11,216
The mechanisms are still those driven by the shaft;

27
00:02:11,236 --> 00:02:14,165
the shaft is now driven by a servo,

28
00:02:14,185 --> 00:02:20,318
and the extra actions are carried out by actuators at given shaft angles.

29
00:02:21,838 --> 00:02:26,495
The shaft servo: three loops and needle positioning.

30
00:02:28,015 --> 00:02:32,804
The shaft motor is a permanent-magnet synchronous motor,

31
00:02:32,824 --> 00:02:37,355
and i_q is the part of its current that makes torque.

32
00:02:37,755 --> 00:02:41,375
The shaft servo nests three control loops.

33
00:02:41,775 --> 00:02:50,566
Innermost is the current loop, the fastest, around 0.1 milliseconds,

34
00:02:50,586 --> 00:02:54,583
holding i_q, and so the torque.

35
00:02:54,983 --> 00:03:01,225
In the middle is the velocity loop, a PI controller.

36
00:03:01,625 --> 00:03:05,725
Outermost is the position loop.

37
00:03:06,125 --> 00:03:10,707
Each outer loop's output is the inner loop's command:

38
00:03:10,727 --> 00:03:16,785
position error becomes a speed command, speed error a current command.

39
00:03:17,185 --> 00:03:25,098
The velocity-loop bandwidth is roughly K_v over the inertia J:

40
00:03:25,118 --> 00:03:31,495
in the example 0.2 over 6 times 10 to the minus 4,

41
00:03:31,515 --> 00:03:35,205
about 333 radians per second.

42
00:03:35,605 --> 00:03:38,278
The shaft load changes every revolution,

43
00:03:38,298 --> 00:03:42,585
and the velocity loop has to suppress that periodic disturbance.

44
00:03:44,105 --> 00:03:47,591
Needle positioning has two stages.

45
00:03:47,991 --> 00:03:54,177
First the velocity loop slows the shaft to the positioning speed,

46
00:03:54,197 --> 00:04:01,146
200 r/min in the example; then, 45 degrees short of the needle-up target,

47
00:04:01,166 --> 00:04:08,593
the position loop takes over, with its output capped at the positioning speed.

48
00:04:08,993 --> 00:04:16,003
With the standard gains, K_p 200, K_v 0.2 and T_i 12 milliseconds,

49
00:04:16,023 --> 00:04:24,203
it overshoots about 0.1 degrees and is within plus or minus 0.5 degrees in 38

50
00:04:24,223 --> 00:04:25,588
milliseconds.

51
00:04:25,988 --> 00:04:34,456
On the right, the handwheel animation from Virtual Lab 13-1, slowed 20 times,

52
00:04:34,476 --> 00:04:40,408
with the tolerance band magnified 10 times, replaying.

53
00:04:41,928 --> 00:04:48,457
Compare the position-error curves: 500 on the left, 200 on the right;

54
00:04:48,477 --> 00:04:52,348
the lower half magnifies the final stage.

55
00:04:52,748 --> 00:04:55,398
With a position gain of 200,

56
00:04:55,418 --> 00:05:01,978
the overshoot is about 0.1 degrees and it settles in 38 milliseconds.

57
00:05:02,378 --> 00:05:10,550
Raise only that gain to 500 and it overshoots about 1.8 degrees and takes 50

58
00:05:10,570 --> 00:05:11,951
milliseconds:

59
00:05:11,971 --> 00:05:13,998
slower, not faster.

60
00:05:14,398 --> 00:05:19,824
The position loop is now too stiff for the velocity loop to follow,

61
00:05:19,844 --> 00:05:22,181
and the torque hits its peak.

62
00:05:22,581 --> 00:05:28,471
The rule of thumb is a position gain of a quarter to a half of K_v over J:

63
00:05:28,491 --> 00:05:33,263
stiffen the velocity loop first, then add the position loop.

64
00:05:33,663 --> 00:05:40,047
Fit a heavier handwheel and the inertia grows, so the gains must be retuned.

65
00:05:41,567 --> 00:05:45,467
Why does the velocity loop need PI?

66
00:05:45,867 --> 00:05:48,744
At standstill the speed is zero,

67
00:05:48,764 --> 00:05:54,085
and without an integral only the proportional term is left:

68
00:05:54,105 --> 00:05:59,607
motor torque equals K_v times K_p times the position error e.

69
00:06:00,007 --> 00:06:07,076
As long as that torque is below the Coulomb friction T_f, nothing moves,

70
00:06:07,096 --> 00:06:11,507
and the shaft can stop anywhere in that band.

71
00:06:11,907 --> 00:06:22,601
In the example, 0.15 over 0.2 times 200, about 0.21 degrees.

72
00:06:23,001 --> 00:06:27,696
On the right the integral time is set to zero:

73
00:06:27,716 --> 00:06:34,050
watch the error curve stop short of the target and stay there.

74
00:06:34,450 --> 00:06:42,030
An integral slowly builds the small error into torque until the shaft moves.

75
00:06:43,550 --> 00:06:47,336
Encoders and sensors.

76
00:06:48,856 --> 00:06:52,297
The shaft angle comes from an incremental encoder.

77
00:06:52,697 --> 00:06:57,985
Channels A and B are square waves a quarter period apart:

78
00:06:58,005 --> 00:07:05,157
turning forward A leads, turning backward B leads, which gives the direction.

79
00:07:05,557 --> 00:07:11,206
Counting both rising and falling edges gives four counts per period,

80
00:07:11,226 --> 00:07:13,457
called times-four decoding.

81
00:07:13,857 --> 00:07:21,412
A 1024-line encoder then gives 4096 counts per revolution,

82
00:07:21,432 --> 00:07:24,677
about 0.088 degrees each.

83
00:07:25,077 --> 00:07:30,207
An index pulse fixes the absolute angle, and U,

84
00:07:30,227 --> 00:07:37,660
V and W commutation signals let the motor start turning at power-up.

85
00:07:39,180 --> 00:07:44,871
Actuators: steppers, solenoids and power circuits.

86
00:07:46,391 --> 00:07:49,731
Trimming and reverse use solenoids.

87
00:07:50,131 --> 00:07:55,571
When energised, the current rises with time constant L over R;

88
00:07:55,591 --> 00:07:58,741
the force goes with current squared,

89
00:07:58,761 --> 00:08:03,409
and the armature moves only once it beats the spring.

90
00:08:03,809 --> 00:08:11,257
As it moves it generates a back EMF, which puts a notch in the current curve.

91
00:08:11,657 --> 00:08:15,360
A 1-amp hold current alone won't pull it in;

92
00:08:15,380 --> 00:08:19,677
3 amps all the time would dissipate about 70 watts.

93
00:08:20,077 --> 00:08:27,003
The answer is a 24-volt boost that pulls in within 6.9 milliseconds,

94
00:08:27,023 --> 00:08:31,395
then chopping to hold 1 amp, about 8 watts.

95
00:08:31,795 --> 00:08:38,331
On the right the boost time sweeps between 0 and 20 milliseconds;

96
00:08:38,351 --> 00:08:41,962
watch whether the armature pulls in.

97
00:08:43,482 --> 00:08:48,582
At switch-off the coil current needs somewhere to go.

98
00:08:48,982 --> 00:08:53,363
With a diode it decays slowly with L over R,

99
00:08:53,383 --> 00:08:56,963
and release takes 12.9 milliseconds;

100
00:08:56,983 --> 00:09:03,364
with a 48-volt Zener it collapses quickly and release takes 5.1,

101
00:09:03,384 --> 00:09:10,465
at the cost of 72 volts, 24 plus 48, across the transistor at turn-off,

102
00:09:10,485 --> 00:09:14,865
and about 40 millijoules absorbed each time.

103
00:09:15,265 --> 00:09:21,064
Delays convert to shaft angle as theta equals 6 n t:

104
00:09:21,084 --> 00:09:25,987
at 400 r/min one millisecond is 2.4 degrees,

105
00:09:26,007 --> 00:09:31,805
so pull-in is about 17 degrees and release 31 or 12.

106
00:09:32,205 --> 00:09:37,185
On the right the freewheeling voltage sweeps back and forth.

107
00:09:38,705 --> 00:09:47,126
The power circuit: 220 volts rectified gives a DC link of about 310 volts,

108
00:09:47,146 --> 00:09:52,829
and a six-switch inverter bridge drives the motor.

109
00:09:53,229 --> 00:09:57,421
In an emergency stop the motor becomes a generator,

110
00:09:57,441 --> 00:10:00,724
but the rectifier only conducts one way,

111
00:10:00,744 --> 00:10:04,110
so the energy can't go back to the mains;

112
00:10:04,130 --> 00:10:07,249
it can only charge the link capacitor.

113
00:10:07,649 --> 00:10:17,052
At 5000 r/min the kinetic energy is about 82 joules; with 70 percent fed back,

114
00:10:17,072 --> 00:10:26,354
a 470-microfarad capacitor would rise to about 584 volts, which is dangerous.

115
00:10:26,754 --> 00:10:35,748
So the link carries a braking resistor that switches in at about 400 volts

116
00:10:35,768 --> 00:10:38,793
and burns the energy off;

117
00:10:38,813 --> 00:10:45,614
any emergency stop above about 2554 r/min depends on it.

118
00:10:47,134 --> 00:10:55,009
Every joint of a robot arm is a permanent-magnet synchronous motor with an encoder,

119
00:10:55,029 --> 00:11:01,287
and its drive nests the same current, velocity and position loops,

120
00:11:01,307 --> 00:11:06,994
with vector control making joint torque proportional to i_q.

121
00:11:07,394 --> 00:11:14,067
Stopping the shaft at needle-up corresponds to stopping a joint at its target angle:

122
00:11:14,087 --> 00:11:18,768
too much position gain overshoots, and without an integral,

123
00:11:18,788 --> 00:11:21,238
friction leaves a steady error.

124
00:11:21,638 --> 00:11:27,452
When the arm picks up a heavy part its inertia grows and the velocity

125
00:11:27,472 --> 00:11:28,805
bandwidth falls,

126
00:11:28,825 --> 00:11:32,017
just like fitting a heavier handwheel;

127
00:11:32,037 --> 00:11:38,190
that's why robot drives also have a load-inertia ratio and online inertia

128
00:11:38,210 --> 00:11:39,458
identification.

129
00:11:39,858 --> 00:11:44,039
Emergency stops need braking resistors here too,

130
00:11:44,059 --> 00:11:50,253
and gripper valves must be commanded early according to the belt speed.

131
00:11:51,773 --> 00:11:58,593
Needle positioning in everyday life: an elevator levelling at a floor.

132
00:11:58,993 --> 00:12:03,388
As it approaches, it first slows to a low creep speed,

133
00:12:03,408 --> 00:12:09,110
and only a short distance before the levelling point does it switch to

134
00:12:09,130 --> 00:12:11,154
precise position control,

135
00:12:11,174 --> 00:12:15,323
stopping with the car floor flush with the landing.

136
00:12:15,723 --> 00:12:20,898
That's exactly the two-stage method of needle positioning: slow down,

137
00:12:20,918 --> 00:12:23,383
then switch to the position loop.

138
00:12:23,783 --> 00:12:26,614
With more passengers the inertia grows,

139
00:12:26,634 --> 00:12:29,683
and a good elevator controller adapts too.

140
00:12:31,203 --> 00:12:32,743
Back to the start.

141
00:12:33,143 --> 00:12:42,566
At 600 r/min the pull-in takes 24.8 degrees and the diode release 46.4,

142
00:12:42,586 --> 00:12:49,083
both over 20: that's why trimming became erratic.

143
00:12:49,483 --> 00:12:56,338
The 48-volt Zener brings release down to 18.4 degrees,

144
00:12:56,358 --> 00:12:59,648
but pull-in is still 24.8.

145
00:13:00,048 --> 00:13:07,831
The longer pull-in sets the limit: 20 over 6 times 0.0069, about 483 r/min,

146
00:13:07,851 --> 00:13:12,201
so set the trimming speed below about 480,

147
00:13:12,221 --> 00:13:16,987
or raise the drive voltage to shorten pull-in.

148
00:13:17,387 --> 00:13:23,607
At turn-off the transistor sees 72 volts, so choose it with a margin.

149
00:13:25,127 --> 00:13:28,372
